 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Task 2 — Trends and the Spaghetti Problem\n",
    "\n",
    "**Dataset:** `gapminder.csv` — 1,704 rows, 142 countries × 12 years (1952–2007)  \n",
    "Columns: `country`, `continent`, `year`, `lifeExp`, `pop`, `gdpPercap`  \n",
    "No missing values."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import matplotlib.cm as cm\n",
    "\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv('data/gapminder.csv')\n",
    "print(f'Shape: {df.shape}')\n",
    "print(df.head())\n",
    "print(f'\\nMissing values:\\n{df.isnull().sum()}')\n",
    "print(f'\\nCountries: {df[\"country\"].nunique()}, Continents: {df[\"continent\"].unique()}')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 1 — The Messy Spaghetti Chart (Intentionally Bad)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(16, 7))\n",
    "\n",
    "countries = df['country'].unique()\n",
    "colors = cm.tab20(np.linspace(0, 1, len(countries)))\n",
    "\n",
    "for i, country in enumerate(countries):\n",
    "    country_data = df[df['country'] == country].sort_values('year')\n",
    "    ax.plot(country_data['year'], country_data['lifeExp'],\n",
    "            color=colors[i], linewidth=0.8, alpha=0.8, label=country)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_title('Life Expectancy Over Time — All 142 Countries (MESSY — DO NOT COPY)', fontsize=13)\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Year')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Life Expectancy (years)')\n",
    "ax.legend(loc='lower right', fontsize=4, ncol=4)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.savefig('task2_spaghetti_bad.png', dpi=120, bbox_inches='tight')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Everything wrong with this chart:**\n",
    "1. **The legend is unreadable** — 142 entries rendered at 4pt font; no reader can match a line to a country.\n",
    "2. **Colour encodes nothing** — 142 arbitrary colours that cycle and repeat convey no information.\n",
    "3. **The title names the axes, not the finding** — "Life Expectancy Over Time" describes the data, not what the data shows.\n",
    "4. **No single message is possible** — the eye cannot extract a trend or comparison from overlapping spaghetti.\n",
    "5. **Lines are too thin and too numerous** — the visual result is a filled band rather than individual trajectories.\n",
    "6. **The outlier dip (Rwanda, 1992) is invisible** — the most interesting story in the data is lost."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 2a — Fix: Aggregate to One Line per Continent"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "continent_avg = df.groupby(['continent', 'year'])['lifeExp'].mean().reset_index()\n",
    "\n",
    "continent_colors = {\n",
    "    'Africa':   '#E15759',\n",
    "    'Americas': '#4E79A7',\n",
    "    'Asia':     '#F28E2B',\n",
    "    'Europe':   '#59A14F',\n",
    "    'Oceania':  '#B07AA1'\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "\n",
    "for continent, group in continent_avg.groupby('continent'):\n",
    "    group = group.sort_values('year')\n",
    "    ax.plot(group['year'], group['lifeExp'],\n",
    "            color=continent_colors[continent], linewidth=2.5, label=continent, marker='o', markersize=4)\n",
    "    # Label at the end\n",
    "    last = group.iloc[-1]\n",
    "    ax.text(last['year'] + 0.5, last['lifeExp'], continent,\n",
    "            color=continent_colors[continent], fontsize=9, va='center', fontweight='bold')\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_title('All Five Continents Gained Life Expectancy, But Africa Remains Far Behind',\n",
    "             fontsize=12, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Year')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Average Life Expectancy (years)')\n",
    "ax.set_xlim(1950, 2012)\n",
    "ax.set_ylim(30, 82)\n",
    "ax.legend().remove() if ax.get_legend() else None\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.savefig('task2_continent_aggregate.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Why this chart type?**  \n",
    "A line chart is the natural choice for a continuous trend over an ordered time axis. Aggregating to continent reduces 142 lines to 5, making each trajectory legible. Direct labelling replaces the legend, keeping the reader's eye on the lines. Colour is used purposefully — one colour per continent — not decoratively."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 2b — Fix: Highlight 3 Countries of Interest"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "highlight = {\n",
    "    'Rwanda':        '#E15759',   # dramatic dip in 1992 due to genocide\n",
    "    'China':         '#4E79A7',   # steepest rise in Asia\n",
    "    'United States': '#F28E2B',   # high-income reference line\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(11, 6))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Grey all countries\n",
    "for country in df['country'].unique():\n",
    "    data = df[df['country'] == country].sort_values('year')\n",
    "    ax.plot(data['year'], data['lifeExp'], color='#CCCCCC', linewidth=0.6, alpha=0.5, zorder=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Highlight three countries\n",
    "for country, color in highlight.items():\n",
    "    data = df[df['country'] == country].sort_values('year')\n",
    "    ax.plot(data['year'], data['lifeExp'], color=color, linewidth=2.5, zorder=3)\n",
    "    last = data.iloc[-1]\n",
    "    ax.text(last['year'] + 0.5, last['lifeExp'], country,\n",
    "            color=color, fontsize=9, fontweight='bold', va='center')\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Rwanda's 1994 Collapse Stands Out Against a Global Rise in Life Expectancy\",\n",
    "             fontsize=12, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Year')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Life Expectancy (years)')\n",
    "ax.set_xlim(1950, 2012)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.savefig('task2_highlight_countries.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Why this chart type?**  \n",
    "The grey-and-highlight technique preserves the full dataset as context (the reader can see where the highlighted countries sit within the global distribution) while directing attention to exactly three stories. The grey lines are data context, not noise. No legend is needed because labels are placed directly on the lines. Colour encodes identity, not value."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 3 — Written Comparison: Aggregate vs Highlight\n",
    "\n",
    "| | Aggregate (2a) | Highlight (2b) |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **Can show** | Continental patterns and relative gaps between regions | Individual country trajectories, anomalies, outliers |\n",
    "| **Cannot show** | Within-continent variation; individual country stories | Between-continent comparison; aggregate trends |\n",
    "| **Best for** | Answering "which region grew fastest?" | Answering "what happened to Rwanda in 1994?" |\n",
    "\n",
    "**2a (Aggregate)** smooths over individual variation and tells a clean story about group-level trends. It is the right choice when the question is about regions, not countries. Its weakness is that it hides within-continent diversity — for example, Africa's average is dragged down by a few countries while others are doing well.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2b (Highlight)** keeps every country visible as grey context, giving the viewer a sense of the full distribution while focusing attention on selected cases. It is the right choice when a specific country's trajectory is the story. Its weakness is that you are responsible for choosing which three countries matter — a different selection tells a completely different story."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 4 — Has the Gap Between Richest and Poorest Countries Narrowed Since 1952?\n",
    "\n",
    "### First: Inspect the gdpPercap Distribution"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 5))\n",
    "\n",
    "latest = df[df['year'] == df['year'].max()]\n",
    "\n",
    "ax1.hist(latest['gdpPercap'], bins=30, color='steelblue', edgecolor='white')\n",
    "ax1.set_title('GDP per Capita Is Severely Right-Skewed (linear scale)', fontsize=11)\n",
    "ax1.set_xlabel('GDP per Capita (USD)')\n",
    "ax1.set_ylabel('Number of Countries')\n",
    "ax1.set_xlim(left=0)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax2.hist(np.log10(latest['gdpPercap']), bins=30, color='steelblue', edgecolor='white')\n",
    "ax2.set_title('Log Scale Reveals a Near-Normal Distribution', fontsize=11)\n",
    "ax2.set_xlabel('log₁₀(GDP per Capita)')\n",
    "ax2.set_ylabel('Number of Countries')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.savefig('task2_gdp_histogram.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "print('Decision: use log scale for GDP plots — the raw scale compresses nearly all countries into one corner.')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Compute P10 (poorest 10%) and P90 (richest 10%) per year\n",
    "gap = df.groupby('year')['gdpPercap'].agg(\n",
    "    p10=lambda x: x.quantile(0.10),\n",
    "    p50='median',\n",
    "    p90=lambda x: x.quantile(0.90)\n",
    ").reset_index()\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.fill_between(gap['year'], gap['p10'], gap['p90'],\n",
    "                alpha=0.15, color='steelblue', label='P10–P90 range')\n",
    "ax.plot(gap['year'], gap['p90'], color='#E15759', linewidth=2, label='Richest 10% (P90)')\n",
    "ax.plot(gap['year'], gap['p50'], color='#4E79A7', linewidth=2, linestyle='--', label='Median')\n",
    "ax.plot(gap['year'], gap['p10'], color='#76B7B2', linewidth=2, label='Poorest 10% (P10)')\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_yscale('log')\n",
    "ax.set_title('The Rich-Poor GDP Gap Has Widened in Absolute Terms Since 1952\\n(log scale — ratio between P90 and P10 held roughly steady)',\n",
    "             fontsize=11, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Year')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('GDP per Capita (log scale, USD)')\n",
    "ax.legend(loc='upper left')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Annotate ratio\n",
    "for year in [1952, 2007]:\n",
    "    row = gap[gap['year'] == year].iloc[0]\n",
    "    ratio = row['p90'] / row['p10']\n",
    "    ax.annotate(f'Ratio P90/P10 = {ratio:.0f}×',\n",
    "                xy=(year, row['p90']),\n",
    "                xytext=(year, row['p90'] * 1.5),\n",
    "                arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle='->', color='black'),\n",
    "                fontsize=9, ha='center')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.savefig('task2_gdp_gap.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Why a line chart with shaded band?**  \n",
    "I chose a line chart because the question asks about *change over time* — a trend question. A scatter plot of all countries in all years would be too dense. Instead I summarise the distribution each year with three percentiles (P10, P50, P90) and shade the range between them. The log scale is essential: on a linear scale the P90 line would tower over the P10 line and the median would appear to hug the floor, making it impossible to read P10's trend. On a log scale, equal vertical distance means equal *percentage* change, which is the meaningful measure of relative wealth. The annotated P90/P10 ratio at 1952 and 2007 directly answers the question."
   ]
  }
 ]
}
